00:12:30,565 --> 00:12:33,025
He said it was the scariest
thing he's ever had in his life.

238
00:12:34,025 --> 00:12:34,455
Why?

239
00:12:34,925 --> 00:12:36,755
One of them trains every year.

240
00:12:37,645 --> 00:12:38,535
He knows what's happening.

241
00:12:39,095 --> 00:12:40,635
The other one had done no training.

242
00:12:41,045 --> 00:12:44,075
First time he had to deal with it
was when it was actually happening

243
00:12:44,085 --> 00:12:45,485
and his life was in danger.

244
00:12:46,485 --> 00:12:47,745
We can also look closer to home.

245
00:12:47,975 --> 00:12:49,735
Security teams are brilliant at this.

246
00:12:50,055 --> 00:12:53,415
They red team their own s-
own systems all the time.

247
00:12:54,065 --> 00:12:55,315
Let's try and break it.

248
00:12:55,375 --> 00:12:56,875
Let's see what happens if it breaks it.

249
00:12:57,185 --> 00:13:01,425
When they, when you do find a
fault, let's be the ones finding

250
00:13:01,425 --> 00:13:04,755
that fault, and then let's test
against it and train against it.

251
00:13:04,755 --> 00:13:07,995
Get another team trying to resist
the attack, red team, blue team.

252
00:13:08,095 --> 00:13:12,265
We can do the same for our infrastructure
and the same for our reliability.

253
00:13:14,045 --> 00:13:16,085
So we need simulator time too.

254
00:13:16,565 --> 00:13:19,695
We need to build a system
that will allow us to do this.

255
00:13:21,555 --> 00:13:24,845
Re- we need to remember that reactive
incident response isn't training, and

256
00:13:24,845 --> 00:13:29,295
it's gonna be even less tra-- like
training when you lose the easy stuff

257
00:13:29,335 --> 00:13:31,275
and you're just into the scary stuff.

258
00:13:32,625 --> 00:13:35,505
So we need to have practice and
make it routine and deliberate,

259
00:13:35,675 --> 00:13:38,675
scheduled, realistic, and aimed
at the faults that actually hurt.

260
00:13:39,355 --> 00:13:41,725
There's no point training
how to fix a Kubernetes fault

261
00:13:41,785 --> 00:13:42,815
if you do not use Kubernetes

262
00:13:43,815 --> 00:13:46,295
We need to learn from all
incidents, not just the bad ones.

263
00:13:47,645 --> 00:13:50,975
Often, the incidents that you
narrowly avoid give you very good

264
00:13:50,975 --> 00:13:56,665
clues of what could happen in the
incidents that you are yet to come.

265
00:13:57,675 --> 00:14:00,995
If you learn from the ones that you
narrowly avoid, you can often prevent

266
00:14:01,315 --> 00:14:05,125
or mitigate the ones that are about
to come And we need to remember that

267
00:14:05,125 --> 00:14:07,375
readiness becomes a property you maintain.

268
00:14:07,925 --> 00:14:12,065
By keeping your incident responders
sharp, you're in a position to

269
00:14:12,065 --> 00:14:15,055
handle it the next time you get
woken up in the middle of the night.

270
00:14:17,025 --> 00:14:19,095
Unfortunately, though,
practice isn't always enough.

271
00:14:19,495 --> 00:14:20,645
There's always the black swan.

272
00:14:21,415 --> 00:14:23,315
Apollo 13 is an example of this.

273
00:14:24,605 --> 00:14:26,275
They practiced what would happen.

274
00:14:27,035 --> 00:14:31,105
Something bad still happened, but
because they knew how it happened, they

275
00:14:31,105 --> 00:14:33,875
could respond to it I live in Portugal.

276
00:14:34,925 --> 00:14:39,075
Eighteen months ago, we lost the power
outage here because there was a minor

277
00:14:39,075 --> 00:14:41,465
incident that triggered something
else, that triggered something

278
00:14:41,465 --> 00:14:43,585
else, that triggered something
else, and it took out the country.

279
00:14:44,495 --> 00:14:46,155
Note, this is not a real photo.

280
00:14:46,535 --> 00:14:50,895
This is clearly fake and AI, but it gives
a good example of what was going on.

281
00:14:51,545 --> 00:14:55,425
There is an industry that does
practice, and it still failed.

282
00:14:56,815 --> 00:14:59,775
And something close to my own heart, I
used to work as a nuclear safety engineer.

283
00:14:59,815 --> 00:15:03,475
That's where my interest in
safety and safety cultures and

284
00:15:03,475 --> 00:15:04,455
practice really comes from.

285
00:15:04,835 --> 00:15:08,995
Nuclear is fantastic at practice,
but then Fukushima happens,

286
00:15:09,955 --> 00:15:10,955
you still have a problem.

287
00:15:12,415 --> 00:15:15,015
Even with the best practice, there
will still be incidents, there

288
00:15:15,015 --> 00:15:16,555
will still be black swan events.

289
00:15:17,215 --> 00:15:19,625
But you can make it better,
you can make it easier.

290
00:15:20,175 --> 00:15:23,865
Going back to NASA, look up
the history of the Apollo 11.

291
00:15:24,375 --> 00:15:28,545
They were coming down, the
computer overloaded, but it

292
00:15:28,545 --> 00:15:30,255
overloaded in the right way.

293
00:15:30,815 --> 00:15:34,885
It was well-designed, and Neil
Armstrong had been properly trained

294
00:15:34,895 --> 00:15:38,305
that when it overloaded in that
way and that alarm was going off,

295
00:15:38,525 --> 00:15:40,595
that was an alarm he could ignore.

296
00:15:42,025 --> 00:15:45,105
Practice for the failures and keep calm.

297
00:15:46,505 --> 00:15:47,895
So how do we build our future?

298
00:15:48,375 --> 00:15:50,615
How do we move forward into this together?

299
00:15:52,025 --> 00:15:53,005
We need two things.

300
00:15:53,445 --> 00:15:56,195
We need better tooling,
and we need sharper people.

301
00:15:57,165 --> 00:16:01,265
Better tooling to help us surface
context faster, to use the incident

302
00:16:01,285 --> 00:16:05,275
history to guide our training, and to
have agents that are designed to take

303
00:16:05,275 --> 00:16:10,495
the load, resolve the minor incidents,
and assist us when things go wrong.

304
00:16:11,855 --> 00:16:13,335
We also need sharper people.

305
00:16:13,885 --> 00:16:15,985
They need to work fluently with agents.

306
00:16:16,265 --> 00:16:18,495
They need to know how to
work with the new tooling.

307
00:16:18,885 --> 00:16:21,675
They need to be practiced in
low-stake scenarios, and they

308
00:16:21,675 --> 00:16:23,595
need to know how their tools fail.

309
00:16:24,995 --> 00:16:26,245
Neither alone is enough.

310
00:16:26,285 --> 00:16:29,865
Without the right tools and the
practiced people, it will still fail.

311
00:16:31,335 --> 00:16:33,625
For me, that means building
the right SRE agent.

312
00:16:34,095 --> 00:16:37,795
It needs to be an autopilot, taking
the routine and well understood away

313
00:16:37,795 --> 00:16:40,215
from you so you don't have that toil.

314
00:16:40,875 --> 00:16:42,185
It also needs to be a co-pilot.

315
00:16:42,185 --> 00:16:48,355
It needs to assist on complex incidents
and allow humans who are still in command

316
00:16:49,255 --> 00:16:51,295
to work more efficiently and effectively.

317
00:16:52,935 --> 00:16:54,535
The goal cannot be to replace.

318
00:16:55,165 --> 00:17:03,015
Those two dichotomies I presented earlier,
the autopilot or as agent devs, human

319
00:17:03,025 --> 00:17:05,195
ops, neither of those is gonna be right.

320
00:17:05,365 --> 00:17:10,665
It's gonna be a mix We need to train
effectively based on our incidents,

321
00:17:10,795 --> 00:17:14,935
looking at what's happened, what's gone
wrong, where humans can make a difference.

322
00:17:15,345 --> 00:17:17,015
We need to know how AI struggles.

323
00:17:17,795 --> 00:17:19,615
Use your coding agents regularly.

324
00:17:20,025 --> 00:17:21,845
Use the SRE agents regularly.

325
00:17:22,285 --> 00:17:25,875
Flag where it does well, where it
does badly, where you can practice.

326
00:17:26,605 --> 00:17:27,885
Learn how to use them.

327
00:17:28,965 --> 00:17:29,725
Where are they good?

328
00:17:30,575 --> 00:17:31,525
Where they fail?

329
00:17:31,785 --> 00:17:33,905
What does it look like when they fail?

330
00:17:35,425 --> 00:17:37,475
And expect the black swans.

331
00:17:37,965 --> 00:17:41,355
Get used to dealing with things that
you do not expect because they'll be the

332
00:17:41,355 --> 00:17:42,705
ones that are gonna come through to you

333
00:17:43,705 --> 00:17:48,235
One example of learning your tools
in a black swan event that I find

334
00:17:48,335 --> 00:17:53,305
particularly interesting, though rather
harrowing, is the Aeroflot Flight 593.

335
00:17:53,305 --> 00:17:55,535
Here was a plane cruising on autopilot.

336
00:17:55,605 --> 00:17:56,695
Everything was fine.

337
00:17:57,225 --> 00:17:59,435
The pilot decided to bring
his son to the controls.

338
00:18:00,405 --> 00:18:01,055
Fair enough.

339
00:18:01,565 --> 00:18:04,525
His son, being a typical sixteen-year-old,
decided to pretend it was a

340
00:18:04,525 --> 00:18:07,675
fighter jet, so started yanking the
controls about all over the place.

341
00:18:08,645 --> 00:18:10,045
The plane starts responding.

342
00:18:10,365 --> 00:18:11,515
The pilot panics.

343
00:18:11,525 --> 00:18:15,375
He starts yanking the controls around to
try and get the tr- plane flat and level.

344
00:18:15,375 --> 00:18:16,675
They run out of altitude.

345
00:18:16,805 --> 00:18:17,405
They crash.

346
00:18:18,575 --> 00:18:19,005
Why?

347
00:18:20,245 --> 00:18:24,225
When you yank the controls on
an autopilot, you disengage it.

348
00:18:25,625 --> 00:18:29,635
If the son or the pilot had
just let go of the controls,

349
00:18:30,685 --> 00:18:32,945
it would have re- been saved.

350
00:18:33,795 --> 00:18:34,675
No accident.

351
00:18:36,045 --> 00:18:38,505
The pilot did not know his to- tools.

352
00:18:38,645 --> 00:18:43,355
He did not know that yanking the
controls disables the autopilot That's

353
00:18:43,365 --> 00:18:45,385
the sort of error that will creep in.

354
00:18:45,905 --> 00:18:50,395
We need to know how and when to work
with and work against the tooling we

355
00:18:50,395 --> 00:18:52,685
have provided and what that means.

356
00:18:54,175 --> 00:18:56,125
So what can you do tomorrow morning?

357
00:18:57,895 --> 00:18:59,755
Use agents to read, not just write.

358
00:18:59,985 --> 00:19:03,215
Pick up a random code base from
somewhere else in your organization

359
00:19:03,695 --> 00:19:05,155
and work out how it works.

360
00:19:06,135 --> 00:19:07,305
Get the agent to look at it.

361
00:19:08,785 --> 00:19:10,295
Rehearse in low-stakes scenarios.

362
00:19:10,605 --> 00:19:11,415
We all know them.

363
00:19:11,445 --> 00:19:15,295
Game days, drills, failure Fridays,
chaos monkeys, all these things.

364
00:19:15,295 --> 00:19:16,485
We all know what we should be doing.

365
00:19:16,875 --> 00:19:19,475
It's going to become more and
more important that we do it.

366
00:19:19,875 --> 00:19:25,575
Get used to using your SRE agents in
incidents in low-stakes environments

367
00:19:27,095 --> 00:19:29,155
and learn how your agents fail.

368
00:19:29,775 --> 00:19:35,165
In particular, when they do
it really confidently, their

369
00:19:35,225 --> 00:19:37,285
confidence could be their downfall.

370
00:19:37,345 --> 00:19:41,555
And if you follow that and don't
understand what that confidence looks like

371
00:19:42,075 --> 00:19:45,065
and how to push back and how to challenge
it, you walk down the wrong path.

372
00:19:46,065 --> 00:19:47,295
How do you prepare for the big one?

373
00:19:48,665 --> 00:19:53,075
Red team, game days, chaos engineering,
AI in the loop, incident drills.

374
00:19:53,295 --> 00:19:54,625
It's all the same stuff.

375
00:19:54,925 --> 00:19:58,295
But main thing is, think
about what could bite you.

376
00:19:59,385 --> 00:20:01,365
What happens if your EC spun goes down?

377
00:20:03,305 --> 00:20:06,265
What happens if your
read replica has failed?

378
00:20:07,725 --> 00:20:10,785
What happens if your Kafka
cu- queue shuts down?

379
00:20:11,785 --> 00:20:12,985
Test on those things.

380
00:20:13,375 --> 00:20:14,515
Test on the hard ones.

381
00:20:15,105 --> 00:20:20,405
Prioritize the things that really scare
you and embrace your new ownership model.

382
00:20:21,355 --> 00:20:25,275
Share it between-- share the
workload between you and the agents.

383
00:20:25,655 --> 00:20:29,345
I love the fact I don't have to do
all these minor PRs, bumping packages,

384
00:20:29,365 --> 00:20:30,765
doing this, doing that, whatever.

385
00:20:31,145 --> 00:20:35,395
It's great But use that
time in a constructive way.

386
00:20:35,955 --> 00:20:37,985
Practice other things.

387
00:20:38,385 --> 00:20:39,685
Work on your reliability.

388
00:20:39,705 --> 00:20:41,335
Improve your testing coverage.

389
00:20:43,155 --> 00:20:45,995
And make sure owning it is
becoming a conscious choice.

390
00:20:46,375 --> 00:20:51,375
As you start gaining more and
more services, focus on how you

391
00:20:51,375 --> 00:20:54,825
can learn those services and make
them more and more useful for you.

392
00:20:56,305 --> 00:20:58,265
Code it, ship it, own it.

393
00:20:59,755 --> 00:21:02,485
The agents will code it and ship it.

394
00:21:03,605 --> 00:21:08,175
You, though, will still be the
one who owns it, especially

395
00:21:08,355 --> 00:21:09,685
at oh three forty-seven.

396
00:21:10,865 --> 00:21:11,185
Thank you.

